DPDK-Style Ring Buffer (C++ std::atomic)
Topic: C++ · Lock-free SPSC · std::atomic · Memory ordering · Templates


Junior scope: mutex version required. Atomic/lock-free version = bonus if asked.


Phase 1 — Code Review  (~20 min)
Find all bugs and quality issues in this SPSC ring buffer implementation.

Code to review
// ring_buffer.hpp  (buggy)
#include <atomic>
#include <array>
#include <cstdint>

template<typename T, std::size_t N>
class RingBuffer {
    std::array<T, N>       buf_;
    std::atomic<uint32_t>  head_{0};   // producer writes
    std::atomic<uint32_t>  tail_{0};   // consumer reads

public:
    bool push(const T& val) {
        uint32_t h    = head_.load();
        uint32_t next = (h + 1) % N;
        if (next == tail_.load()) return false;  // full
        buf_[h] = val;
        head_.store(next);
        return true;
    }

    bool pop(T& val) {
        uint32_t t = tail_.load();
        if (t == head_.load()) return false;     // empty
        val = buf_[t];
        tail_.store((t + 1) % N);
        return true;
    }
};


There are 3 bugs and 2 quality issues. Think about: memory ordering, capacity, cache behaviour.


Answers — Bugs Found
#
Type
Description
1
BUG
Wrong memory order: buf_[h]=val can be reordered AFTER head_.store() by CPU — consumer sees new head before data is written. Need memory_order_release on store, memory_order_acquire when reading other side's index.
2
BUG
True capacity is N-1 not N — one slot is always wasted to distinguish full from empty. Caller expecting N items only gets N-1.
3
BUG
No static_assert that N is power of 2. Code uses % N which is correct but slow. DPDK uses & (N-1) mask which requires power-of-2. Without assertion caller gets slow modulo silently with e.g. N=10.
4
QUALITY
head_ and tail_ are adjacent in memory — on a 64-byte cache line they false-share, causing cache ping-pong between producer/consumer cores. Should be padded with alignas(64).
5
QUALITY
No size() or capacity() method — caller has no way to query buffer state.



Phase 2 — Test Cases  (~20 min)
Write single-threaded tests first to verify logic, then a two-thread SPSC test.

#include <cassert>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>
#include <chrono>
#include "ring_buffer.hpp"

// Test 1 — basic push and pop
void test_basic_push_pop() {
    RingBuffer<int, 8> rb;
    assert(rb.push(42) == true);
    int val = 0;
    assert(rb.pop(val) == true);
    assert(val == 42);
    printf("PASS test_basic_push_pop\n");
}

// Test 2 — pop from empty returns false
void test_pop_empty() {
    RingBuffer<int, 8> rb;
    int val = 0;
    assert(rb.pop(val) == false);
    printf("PASS test_pop_empty\n");
}

// Test 3 — fill to capacity, next push must fail
void test_push_full() {
    RingBuffer<int, 4> rb;   // 3 usable slots
    assert(rb.push(1) == true);
    assert(rb.push(2) == true);
    assert(rb.push(3) == true);
    assert(rb.push(4) == false);  // full
    printf("PASS test_push_full\n");
}

// Test 4 — FIFO order preserved
void test_fifo_order() {
    RingBuffer<int, 8> rb;
    rb.push(10); rb.push(20); rb.push(30);
    int v;
    rb.pop(v); assert(v == 10);
    rb.pop(v); assert(v == 20);
    rb.pop(v); assert(v == 30);
    printf("PASS test_fifo_order\n");
}

// Test 5 — wrap-around: fill, drain, fill again
void test_wrap_around() {
    RingBuffer<int, 4> rb;
    rb.push(1); rb.push(2); rb.push(3);
    int v;
    rb.pop(v); rb.pop(v);       // drain 2
    assert(rb.push(4) == true);
    assert(rb.push(5) == true);
    rb.pop(v); assert(v == 3);
    rb.pop(v); assert(v == 4);
    rb.pop(v); assert(v == 5);
    printf("PASS test_wrap_around\n");
}

// Test 6 — two threads: no lost or duplicated items
void test_threaded_spsc() {
    constexpr int N = 10000;
    RingBuffer<int, 1024> rb;
    std::vector<int> received;
    received.reserve(N);

    std::thread producer([&]() {
        for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i)
            while (!rb.push(i)) {}   // spin until space
    });

    std::thread consumer([&]() {
        int val;
        while ((int)received.size() < N)
            if (rb.pop(val)) received.push_back(val);
    });

    producer.join();
    consumer.join();
    assert((int)received.size() == N);
    for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) assert(received[i] == i);
    printf("PASS test_threaded_spsc\n");
}

int main() {
    test_basic_push_pop();
    test_pop_empty();
    test_push_full();
    test_fifo_order();
    test_wrap_around();
    test_threaded_spsc();
    printf("All tests passed.\n");
}



Phase 3 — Implementation  (~25 min)
Fix all bugs. Key things: memory_order on every atomic op, alignas(64) padding, power-of-2 static_assert.

Mutex version — write this cold (junior baseline)
#pragma once
#include <array>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <cstddef>

template<typename T, std::size_t N>
class RingBuffer {
    std::array<T, N>        buf_;
    std::size_t             head_{0};
    std::size_t             tail_{0};
    std::size_t             count_{0};
    mutable std::mutex      mtx_;
    std::condition_variable not_full_;
    std::condition_variable not_empty_;

public:
    void push(const T& val) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx_);
        not_full_.wait(lock, [this]{ return count_ < N; });
        buf_[head_] = val;
        head_ = (head_ + 1) % N;
        ++count_;
        not_empty_.notify_one();
    }

    T pop() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx_);
        not_empty_.wait(lock, [this]{ return count_ > 0; });
        T val    = buf_[tail_];
        tail_    = (tail_ + 1) % N;
        --count_;
        not_full_.notify_one();
        return val;
    }

    bool try_push(const T& val) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx_);
        if (count_ == N) return false;
        buf_[head_] = val;
        head_ = (head_ + 1) % N;
        ++count_;
        not_empty_.notify_one();
        return true;
    }

    bool try_pop(T& val) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx_);
        if (count_ == 0) return false;
        val  = buf_[tail_];
        tail_ = (tail_ + 1) % N;
        --count_;
        not_full_.notify_one();
        return true;
    }

    std::size_t size() const {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx_);
        return count_;
    }
};


Lock-free SPSC version — bonus if asked
#pragma once
#include <atomic>
#include <array>
#include <cstdint>

// Pad to 64-byte cache line — prevents false sharing between cores
struct alignas(64) PaddedAtomic {
    std::atomic<uint32_t> val{0};
};

template<typename T, std::size_t N>
class SPSCRingBuffer {
    static_assert((N & (N - 1)) == 0, "N must be power of 2");
    static constexpr uint32_t MASK = static_cast<uint32_t>(N - 1u);

    std::array<T, N> buf_;
    PaddedAtomic     head_;   // written by producer only
    PaddedAtomic     tail_;   // written by consumer only

public:
    // Producer thread only
    bool push(const T& val) {
        uint32_t h    = head_.val.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
        uint32_t next = (h + 1u) & MASK;
        // acquire: see all consumer writes up to this tail value
        if (next == tail_.val.load(std::memory_order_acquire))
            return false;  // full
        buf_[h] = val;
        // release: buf_ write is visible before head update
        head_.val.store(next, std::memory_order_release);
        return true;
    }

    // Consumer thread only
    bool pop(T& val) {
        uint32_t t = tail_.val.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
        // acquire: see all producer writes up to this head value
        if (t == head_.val.load(std::memory_order_acquire))
            return false;  // empty
        val = buf_[t];
        // release: buf_ read is done before tail update
        tail_.val.store((t + 1u) & MASK, std::memory_order_release);
        return true;
    }

    std::size_t capacity() const { return N - 1u; }
};


Memory order — one sentence each (memorize this)
relaxed  → just atomicity, no ordering. Use for reading your OWN index.
acquire  → see everything the other side wrote before its release.
release  → my data write is done — other side can safely acquire now.


Follow-up questions — be ready to answer
What is false sharing? — Two threads write different variables on the same 64-byte cache line — causes cache invalidation between cores even though they don't share data.
Why alignas(64)? — Forces head_ and tail_ onto separate cache lines so producer and consumer don't invalidate each other's cache.
Why relaxed on local load but acquire when reading the other side? — You only need ordering guarantees when synchronizing with the other thread's writes.
DPDK rte_ring uses 4 pointers (prod_head/tail + cons_head/tail) — why? — To support MPMC (multi-producer multi-consumer). Separating head and tail allows multiple producers to reserve slots concurrently.
Mutex vs lock-free — when to choose each? — Mutex: simpler, correct, use by default. Lock-free: only when profiling proves mutex is the bottleneck (e.g. 10Gbps packet path).

